---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "Backtesting — And the Ways It Lies to You";
const description =
  "What a backtest is, the specific errors that inflate results, and a worked " +
  "example of a backtest that reported Sharpe 3.2 when the real figure was 1.1.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Trading / Backtesting">
  <h1>Backtesting</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A backtest applies a set of rules to historical prices and reports what would
    have happened. Done carefully it is the only way to check whether an idea has
    ever worked. Done carelessly — which is the norm — it produces a number that
    is mostly an artefact of how it was measured.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    This page is written from a specific example: a real backtest that reported a
    Sharpe ratio above 3, where the honest figure turned out to be near 1.1. The
    difference was not the strategy. It was the measurement.
  </p>

  <h2>The errors that inflate results</h2>

  <h3>1. No capital constraint</h3>
  <p>
    The most damaging and the least discussed. A backtest that runs each stock
    independently and then pools the results is implicitly assuming a separate
    account per stock.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    In the example, pooling per-stock results implied about <strong>115
    simultaneous open positions</strong> and roughly <strong>2,200% of capital
    deployed</strong>, on 99% of trading days. There is no such account. Replaying
    the identical trades against a single realistic account — one pot of money,
    fifteen positions, no leverage — is what took the Sharpe from 3.2 to 1.1.
  </p>
  <p>
    It also surfaced something the original could not express: only about
    <strong>one signal in eight could actually be funded</strong>. That turns the
    question from "which signals are good" into "which fifteen do I take today",
    which is a completely different problem.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Annualising trade returns as if they were sequential</h3>
  <p>
    A common formula annualises by the square root of trades per year. That is
    valid for trades taken one after another. It is wrong when positions overlap,
    because correlated simultaneous trades are not independent observations —
    and it overstates the result by roughly the square root of the number of
    concurrent positions.
  </p>

  <h3>3. Survivorship bias</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Building a universe from currently listed companies quietly excludes every
    firm that failed. The backtest is then run on a population pre-filtered for
    survival, which is exactly the filter you do not have in advance.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Lookahead, including the subtle kind</h3>
  <p>
    The obvious form is using tomorrow's price today. The subtle forms are worse
    because they look reasonable:
  </p>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>ranking_by_an_in_sample_model</code><span class="meta">real example</span></div>
      <p>Using a model trained on the same trades to decide which trades to take.
        The model is picking winners it was already shown the answers to. In the
        example this produced a <strong>6,473% return</strong> — entirely
        fictional. The fix is to score each trade only with a model trained on
        data that closed before it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>pretrained_weights</code><span class="meta">modern version</span></div>
      <p>Any model trained on market history overlapping your test window has
        seen the answers. The leak is in the parameters, not the inputs, so
        careful feature construction does not catch it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>survivorship_in_the_universe_file</code><span class="meta">easy to miss</span></div>
      <p>Today's index constituents applied to a ten-year backtest embeds a
        decade of hindsight about which companies would still be there.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>5. Costs that are optimistic</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    In the example the strategy returned roughly 15% a year at 0.20% round-trip
    costs, and <strong>less than the index</strong> at 1.0% — which is realistic
    for illiquid smallcaps. The break-even was near 0.9%. An edge that only exists
    at institutional fill quality is not an edge you have.
  </p>

  <h2>The check that catches most of it</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Split your history in two. Choose every parameter using only the first half.
    Then run the second half once, without changing anything. If the result
    collapses, you fitted the past.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the example, re-optimising the parameters produced <strong>+21%
    in-sample</strong> and <strong>+2% out-of-sample</strong>. That gap is the
    overfitting, measured. See <a href="/learn/overfitting/">overfitting</a> for
    why searching harder makes this worse rather than better.
  </p>

  <h2>What a believable backtest looks like</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>one_account</code><span class="meta">non-negotiable</span></div>
      <p>A single pot of money, a cap on concurrent positions, and a rule for
        which signals get funded when more fire than you can afford.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>daily_equity_curve</code><span class="meta">not trade returns</span></div>
      <p>Marked to market daily, so drawdown reflects the pain of holding rather
        than only realised losses.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>costs_and_a_liquidity_floor</code><span class="meta">both</span></div>
      <p>Realistic slippage, and a minimum turnover so you are not backtesting
        fills you could never get.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>modest_numbers</code><span class="meta">the tell</span></div>
      <p>A single honest equity signal is Sharpe 0.3 to 0.8. Anything above 2
        from one rule on daily bars is a measurement error until proven
        otherwise. Implausibly good results are evidence of a bug, not of skill.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>Why this page exists</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Almost everyone publishes the version of their backtest that looks best.
    The numbers above are what the same strategy looked like before and after the
    measurement errors were fixed — 3.2 became 1.1, and the strategy did not
    change at all. The 1.1 was always the true figure; what was removed was an
    artefact sitting on top of it.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    A Sharpe of 1.1 that survives honest accounting is worth far more than a 3.2
    that does not. If your backtest looks extraordinary, the most likely
    explanation is that it is measuring something other than what you think.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/overfitting/">overfitting</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/risk-management/">risk management</a> ·
    <a href="/methodology/">how this site computes its data</a>
  </p>
</Base>
